    1  -   first_light              0.003   100.00   1.458e+03  info
          L_si first exceeds 1e-6 of its peak. This is just a visibility threshold; the real value
          depends on your sensor floor and on where the simulation starts (zstart).
    2  A   ablation_onset           1.278    85.27   2.046e+04  stylized
          Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on
          (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's
          light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~85 km as low-confidence.
          Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin,
          volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
    3  Y   first_yield              2.763    68.18   7.511e+05  physical
          Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 0.04 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and
          heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5
          / H_f in [R22]).
    4  F   flare                    2.784    67.95   1.537e+06  stylized
          Light rises x4.2 in 0.12 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with:
          yield gen 0 at 68.1 km (100% of m0); split gen 0 at 67.6 km (99% of m0). Dust groups supply
          25% of the rise and 19% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare
          (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic
          cascade.
    5  B   first_breakup            2.808    67.67   3.170e+06  stylized
          Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as
          dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of
          a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification.
          Light grew x4.2 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
    6  P   peak                     4.731    46.48   1.500e+07  physical
          Maximum light. It sits ~+3.2 km from the peak of energy deposition (43.2 km) because the
          luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 17.1 km/s).
    7  -   drag_overtakes_ablation   5.400    40.26   9.133e+06  stylized
          Drag work now exceeds the kinetic energy carried off by ablated mass. The ratio
          ablation/drag is about Lambda v^2/(2 Q Cd), so this happens when the speed falls below ~14
          km/s (from the model's heating law).
    8  D   rolloff                  5.658    38.21   5.515e+06  physical
          Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 0.93 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used
          up (speed 65% of v0, 34% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue
